---
source: crates/qr-cli/tests/payload.rs
expression: wifi_help
---
Encode WiFi credentials that phones can join with one tap

Usage: qr wifi [OPTIONS] --ssid <SSID>

Options:
      --ssid <SSID>
          The network name

      --password <PASSWORD>
          The password. It is visible in shell history and the process list; prefer --password-stdin

      --password-stdin
          Read the password from stdin (one trailing newline is dropped)

      --auth <AUTH>
          How the network authenticates: wpa (WPA/WPA2/WPA3), wep or nopass (an open network)
          
          [default: wpa]

      --hidden
          The network does not broadcast its name

      --print-payload
          Print the payload text instead of a code (with a trailing newline, so `... --print-payload
          | qr` encodes exactly the same data). A WiFi password is printed too, since the code holds
          it

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour; NO_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE are honoured in auto

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour when writing to a terminal, unless the environment says not to
          - always: Always colour
          - never:  Never colour
          
          [default: auto]

  -q, --quiet
          Print errors only, no warnings

  -v, --verbose
          Also print what was encoded and how

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Render options:
  -o, --output <PATH|->
          Write the code to PATH ('-' for stdout, not /dev/stdout). Existing files are replaced
          atomically and keep their permissions; a symlink is replaced, not followed

  -f, --format <FORMAT>
          Output format [default: from the -o extension; else terminal when stdout is a terminal,
          otherwise text]

          Possible values:
          - terminal: Unicode half blocks, forced black on white with ANSI colours
          - text:     Unicode half blocks with no escape sequences
          - ascii:    '##' and spaces, pure ASCII
          - png:      PNG image (1-bit indexed, or RGBA when transparent)
          - svg:      Scalable Vector Graphics
          - jpeg:     JPEG image (quality 92; at least 4 pixels per module)
          - webp:     Lossless WebP image
          - pdf:      One-page vector PDF
          - eps:      Encapsulated PostScript

      --ec <LEVEL>
          Error correction level: L, M, Q or H
          
          [default: M]

      --qr-version <N>
          Smallest symbol version to use, 1 to 40 (larger data still grows it)

      --mode <MODE>
          Data mode: auto, numeric, alnum or byte
          
          [default: auto]

      --mask <N>
          Force mask pattern 0 to 7 instead of picking one automatically

      --margin <N>
          Quiet zone around the code, in modules (below 4 may not scan)
          
          [default: 4]

      --scale <N>
          Size of one module in pixels (PNG, JPEG, WebP, SVG) or points (PDF, EPS) [default: 10]

      --size <PX>
          Width and height of a PNG, JPEG or WebP image in pixels, with the largest whole module
          size that fits (vector formats are at most this size)

      --dpi <N>
          Pixel density to record in a PNG or JPEG file, in dots per inch

      --fg <COLOR>
          Colour of dark modules: #RGB, #RRGGBB, #RRGGBBAA or a CSS name [default: black]

      --bg <COLOR>
          Colour of light modules and the quiet zone [default: white]

      --transparent
          Leave the background unpainted (image formats only)

      --invert
          Swap dark and light modules (or --fg and --bg for images)

      --shape <SHAPE>
          How dark modules are drawn in png, jpeg, webp, svg, pdf and eps output; the three finder
          patterns stay square

          Possible values:
          - square:  Full squares: the standard look
          - rounded: Squares with their exposed corners rounded
          - dots:    Circles
          
          [default: square]

      --verify
          Read the rendered code back with a decoder and fail (exit 6) unless it holds the input.
          Automatic for images with a non-default --shape/--fg/--bg, --transparent or --invert. Text
          output is checked as a plain module grid (the symbol, not the characters)

      --no-verify
          Skip the automatic read-back check

      --data-uri
          Print the image as a base64 data: URI instead of raw bytes [default format: svg]. With -o,
          the file holds the URI: use a .txt name

      --no-clobber
          Fail instead of replacing an existing -o file

      --json
          Print a JSON result object on stdout and JSON errors on stderr

Examples:
  qr wifi --ssid Home --password-stdin < pw.txt -o wifi.png
                                    Read the password from a file
  printf '%s' "$PW" | qr wifi --ssid Home --password-stdin
                                    Pipe it from a variable
  qr wifi --ssid 'Cafe Guest' --auth nopass
                                    An open network with no password
  qr wifi --ssid Lab --auth wep --password-stdin --hidden < key.txt

--print-payload shows the password on screen, as the code itself holds it.
